TESTSUITE "ACCTMNT"

*> ACCTMNT is a daily account-maintenance batch program driven by parm
*> cards (SYSIN): RATE changes an account type's interest rate, FEES
*> assesses the monthly minimum-balance fee across every open account
*> (DB2 cursor loop), DORM flags accounts dormant past a parm-supplied
*> day threshold, and anything else is an invalid-function error. Each
*> DB2 UPDATE/INSERT is independently committed or rolled back per parm
*> card / per fetched row.
*>
*> NOTE ON TEST GRANULARITY: these cases PERFORM the individual 1000/
*> 2000/2100/2200/2300/3000 paragraphs directly instead of driving the
*> whole run through 0000-MAIN-PROCESS. That's a deliberate style choice
*> (matching mockymock's own DEPTMQP/CTRRPT reference examples) for
*> finer-grained, more business-meaningful assertions -- it is no longer
*> a workaround for anything: 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING's cursor-open-
*> failure path used to GO TO 2290-FEE-DONE without ever being PERFORMed
*> THRU it, so taking that branch fell through every paragraph physically
*> below 2290-FEE-DONE and off the end of the PROCEDURE DIVISION. Fixed
*> upstream by adding THRU 2290-FEE-DONE to the PERFORM in
*> 2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS; see the cursor-open-failure case in the
*> 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING block below, which now exercises that branch
*> directly and safely.

*> FIXED BUG: neither SELECT clause in the ENVIRONMENT DIVISION used to
*> carry a FILE STATUS IS clause, so WS-FILE-STATUS was never actually
*> populated by the real OPEN statements -- the "IF NOT WS-FILE-OK" guard
*> right after both OPENs was evaluating a field OPEN never touched, so
*> the batch job aborted with RETURN-CODE 16 on every run even when both
*> files opened cleanly. Fixed upstream by adding
*> FILE STATUS IS WS-FILE-STATUS to both SELECT clauses. This case now
*> proves the guard does the right thing on a genuine OPEN failure. Both
*> SELECTs share the one WS-FILE-STATUS field (WSCOMMON's common idiom
*> in this corpus), so whichever OPEN runs *last* is the status the
*> guard actually sees -- the failure has to land on ACCTMRPT (the
*> second OPEN) for this case to exercise the guard at all.
TESTCASE "1000-INITIALIZE aborts with RETURN-CODE 16 when a file genuinely fails to open"
    MOCK OPEN PARMFILE
        MOVE "00" TO WS-FILE-STATUS
    END-MOCK
    MOCK OPEN ACCTMRPT
        MOVE "35" TO WS-FILE-STATUS
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    MOCK READ PARMFILE
        SET WS-EOF TO TRUE
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 1000-INITIALIZE
    EXPECT RETURN-CODE TO BE 16
    VERIFY OPEN PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY OPEN ACCTMRPT WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY WRITE RPT-RECORD WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY READ PARMFILE WAS CALLED NEVER

*> Now that FILE STATUS IS WS-FILE-STATUS is wired up, a mocked OPEN
*> moving "00" (simulating a real successful open) correctly clears the
*> guard and lets the rest of the paragraph run -- this is what actually
*> happens in production today, not a workaround.
TESTCASE "1000-INITIALIZE writes the report header and primes the first read once past the guard"
    MOCK OPEN PARMFILE
        MOVE "00" TO WS-FILE-STATUS
    END-MOCK
    MOCK OPEN ACCTMRPT
        MOVE "00" TO WS-FILE-STATUS
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    MOCK READ PARMFILE
        MOVE "RATE" TO WS-PARM-FUNC
        MOVE "SAV" TO WS-PARM-ACCT-TYPE
        MOVE 4.750000 TO WS-PARM-NEW-RATE
        MOVE 180 TO WS-PARM-DORM-DAYS
        MOVE "OPR00001" TO WS-PARM-USER-ID
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 1000-INITIALIZE
    EXPECT WS-PARM-FUNC TO BE "RATE"
    EXPECT WS-PARM-ACCT-TYPE TO BE "SAV"
    VERIFY OPEN PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY OPEN ACCTMRPT WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY WRITE RPT-RECORD WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY READ PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE

*> 2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS: EVALUATE dispatch on the parm card already
*> loaded into WS-PARM-RECORD. Happy path: a RATE card routes to
*> 2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE exactly once.
TESTCASE "2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS dispatches a RATE card to 2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
        MOVE 30 TO SQLERRD(3)
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    MOCK READ PARMFILE
        SET WS-EOF TO TRUE
    END-MOCK
    MOVE "RATE" TO WS-PARM-FUNC
    MOVE "CHK" TO WS-PARM-ACCT-TYPE
    MOVE 2.250000 TO WS-PARM-NEW-RATE
    MOVE "OPR00002" TO WS-PARM-USER-ID
    PERFORM 2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS
    EXPECT WS-RECORD-COUNT TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-RATES-CHANGED TO BE 30
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "OPEN" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY READ PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE

*> Error path: an unrecognized WS-PARM-FUNC must fall through to
*> WHEN OTHER and never touch any of the three real functions' boundaries
*> -- proves the EVALUATE dispatch guard, not just static reachability.
TESTCASE "2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS flags an unrecognized function code without dispatching"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    MOCK READ PARMFILE
        SET WS-EOF TO TRUE
    END-MOCK
    MOVE "XXXX" TO WS-PARM-FUNC
    PERFORM 2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-RECORD-COUNT TO BE 1
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "OPEN" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY READ PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE

*> 2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE happy path: DB2 accepts the mass UPDATE for
*> the given account type; SQLERRD(3) (rows-affected) accumulates into
*> WS-RATES-CHANGED and the unit of work commits.
TESTCASE "2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE applies a rate change and commits"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
        MOVE 128 TO SQLERRD(3)
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOVE "SAV" TO WS-PARM-ACCT-TYPE
    MOVE 4.750000 TO WS-PARM-NEW-RATE
    MOVE "OPR00003" TO WS-PARM-USER-ID
    PERFORM 2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE
    EXPECT HV-ACCT-TYPE TO BE "SAV"
    EXPECT HV-NEW-RATE TO BE 4.750000
    EXPECT WS-RATES-CHANGED TO BE 128
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "ROLLBACK" WAS CALLED NEVER

*> Error path: DB2 rejects the UPDATE (e.g. a deadlock) -- the unit of
*> work must roll back instead of commit, and the failure is counted,
*> not silently absorbed.
TESTCASE "2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE rolls back and counts an error when DB2 rejects the update"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET INTEREST_RATE"
        MOVE -803 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOVE "CDA" TO WS-PARM-ACCT-TYPE
    MOVE 3.100000 TO WS-PARM-NEW-RATE
    MOVE "OPR00004" TO WS-PARM-USER-ID
    PERFORM 2100-PROCESS-RATE-CHANGE
    EXPECT WS-RATES-CHANGED TO BE 0
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 1
    VERIFY SQL "ROLLBACK" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED NEVER

*> 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING happy path: the fetched account's balance is
*> below its minimum and it carries a monthly fee, so 2220/2230 post a
*> DR transaction and debit both balances by the fee amount. Driven
*> THRU 2290-FEE-DONE like the upstream call site (see the bug note
*> further down), so the cursor closes once after the loop.
TESTCASE "2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING posts a monthly fee when the balance is below the minimum"
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH" ROWS
        ( HV-ACCT-NUMBER = "ACCT00000101", HV-ACCT-TYPE = "CHK", HV-ACCT-STATUS = "O", HV-CIF-NUMBER = "CIF0000101", HV-INTEREST-RATE = 0.010000, HV-CURRENT-BALANCE = 450.00, HV-AVAILABLE-BALANCE = 450.00, HV-LAST-ACTIVITY-DATE = "2026-07-15", HV-MONTHLY-FEE = 12.00, HV-MIN-BALANCE = 500.00 )
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING THRU 2290-FEE-DONE
    EXPECT WS-ACCTS-READ TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-FEES-POSTED TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 0
    EXPECT WS-COMMIT-COUNT TO BE 1
    EXPECT HV-CURRENT-BALANCE TO BE 438.00
    EXPECT HV-AVAILABLE-BALANCE TO BE 438.00
    VERIFY SQL "FETCH" WAS CALLED 2 TIMES
    VERIFY SQL "INSERT" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY WRITE RPT-RECORD WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "CLOSE" WAS CALLED ONCE

*> Edge path: the fetched account's balance is at/above its minimum, so
*> the fee-posting guard (2220) must skip both the INSERT and the
*> balance UPDATE entirely.
TESTCASE "2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING skips the fee when the balance is not below the minimum"
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH" ROWS
        ( HV-ACCT-NUMBER = "ACCT00000102", HV-ACCT-TYPE = "SAV", HV-ACCT-STATUS = "O", HV-CIF-NUMBER = "CIF0000102", HV-INTEREST-RATE = 0.500000, HV-CURRENT-BALANCE = 5000.00, HV-AVAILABLE-BALANCE = 5000.00, HV-LAST-ACTIVITY-DATE = "2026-08-01", HV-MONTHLY-FEE = 12.00, HV-MIN-BALANCE = 500.00 )
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING THRU 2290-FEE-DONE
    EXPECT WS-ACCTS-READ TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-FEES-POSTED TO BE 0
    EXPECT HV-CURRENT-BALANCE TO BE 5000.00
    VERIFY SQL "INSERT" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY WRITE RPT-RECORD WAS CALLED NEVER

*> Error path: the cursor opens fine but the FETCH itself errors out --
*> the paragraph must count the error and force the loop to stop
*> (MOVE +100 TO SQLCODE) instead of looping forever. Stays inside the
*> ordinary "PERFORM ... UNTIL" loop (no GO TO involved), so unlike the
*> cursor-open-failure scenario below it is safe to drive directly.
TESTCASE "2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING counts a fetch error and stops the loop"
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH"
        MOVE -911 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING THRU 2290-FEE-DONE
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-ACCTS-READ TO BE 0
    EXPECT WS-FEES-POSTED TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "FETCH" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "INSERT" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "CLOSE" WAS CALLED ONCE

*> FIXED BUG: when the cursor OPEN fails, 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING does
*> "GO TO 2290-FEE-DONE" to close the cursor. 2290-FEE-DONE is a separate
*> paragraph, and the call site in 2000-PROCESS-PARM-CARDS used to
*> PERFORM 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING bare (no THRU), so taking this GO TO
*> for real fell through the rest of the source file in physical order
*> and off the end of the PROCEDURE DIVISION, silently ending the whole
*> program. Fixed upstream by adding THRU 2290-FEE-DONE to that PERFORM.
*> This TESTCASE mirrors that fix locally (PERFORM ... THRU 2290-FEE-DONE)
*> so the branch can be driven safely and directly: the cursor never gets
*> fetched from, but it does still get closed via 2290-FEE-DONE.
TESTCASE "2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING counts an error and closes the cursor when OPEN fails"
    MOCK SQL "OPEN"
        MOVE -911 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "FETCH"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "INSERT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET CURRENT_BALANCE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "CLOSE"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK WRITE RPT-RECORD
    END-MOCK
    PERFORM 2200-PROCESS-FEE-POSTING THRU 2290-FEE-DONE
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 1
    EXPECT WS-ACCTS-READ TO BE 0
    EXPECT WS-FEES-POSTED TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "FETCH" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "INSERT" WAS CALLED NEVER
    VERIFY SQL "CLOSE" WAS CALLED ONCE

*> 2300-PROCESS-DORMANCY-CHECK happy path: DB2 accepts the mass UPDATE
*> flagging every stale CHK/SAV/MMA account dormant; SQLERRD(3) accumulates
*> into WS-DORM-FLAGGED and the unit of work commits.
TESTCASE "2300-PROCESS-DORMANCY-CHECK flags accounts inactive past the dormancy threshold"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS"
        MOVE 0 TO SQLCODE
        MOVE 47 TO SQLERRD(3)
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOVE 180 TO WS-PARM-DORM-DAYS
    PERFORM 2300-PROCESS-DORMANCY-CHECK
    EXPECT WS-DORM-FLAGGED TO BE 47
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "ROLLBACK" WAS CALLED NEVER

*> Error path: DB2 rejects the dormancy UPDATE -- must roll back and
*> count the error instead of committing a partial/no-op change.
TESTCASE "2300-PROCESS-DORMANCY-CHECK rolls back and counts an error when DB2 rejects the update"
    MOCK SQL "UPDATE BANK. ACCOUNT SET ACCT_STATUS"
        MOVE -911 TO SQLCODE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK SQL "ROLLBACK"
    END-MOCK
    MOVE 90 TO WS-PARM-DORM-DAYS
    PERFORM 2300-PROCESS-DORMANCY-CHECK
    EXPECT WS-DORM-FLAGGED TO BE 0
    EXPECT WS-ERRORS TO BE 1
    VERIFY SQL "ROLLBACK" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED NEVER

*> 3000-FINALIZE: the batch's own exit-code contract -- any error counted
*> during the run must surface as a non-zero RETURN-CODE for the job
*> scheduler, even though every individual unit of work already committed
*> or rolled back on its own.
TESTCASE "3000-FINALIZE sets a non-zero return code when errors occurred"
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK CLOSE PARMFILE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK CLOSE ACCTMRPT
    END-MOCK
    MOVE 3 TO WS-ERRORS
    PERFORM 3000-FINALIZE
    EXPECT RETURN-CODE TO BE 4
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY CLOSE PARMFILE WAS CALLED ONCE
    VERIFY CLOSE ACCTMRPT WAS CALLED ONCE

*> Happy path: a clean run (no errors counted) must exit 0.
TESTCASE "3000-FINALIZE sets a zero return code when the run is clean"
    MOCK SQL "COMMIT"
    END-MOCK
    MOCK CLOSE PARMFILE
    END-MOCK
    MOCK CLOSE ACCTMRPT
    END-MOCK
    MOVE 0 TO WS-ERRORS
    PERFORM 3000-FINALIZE
    EXPECT RETURN-CODE TO BE 0
    VERIFY SQL "COMMIT" WAS CALLED ONCE
